# i-shell (L=6) cross-CODE reference input for the Cartesian->spherical transform.
# Guards FU2: L=6 uses the recurrence oracle directly and has never been exercised
# end-to-end against another code. MAX_L=6 makes this the top of the supported range.
# The pre-existing high-L gates are cross-ENGINE (all engines share the transform,
# so they agreed with each other while all being wrong vs an external reference).
# See docs/SPHERICAL_F_SHELL_ACCURACY_SCOPE.md FU1/FU2/FU4.
# NB high-L bases are near-linear-dependent: spherical only, generous cycles.
#
# NOT REGISTERED as a regression case: cc-pV6Z on Ne is 140 spherical AOs, and the
# conventional nb^4 ERI build makes this far too heavy for the suite (cc-pV5Z, at 91
# AOs, already takes 41 s). Kept as a runnable input so FU2 -- the first end-to-end
# exercise of the L=6 recurrence-oracle path against another code -- can be done
# deliberately rather than re-derived. The PySCF reference, computed from Planck's
# own cc-pV6Z GBS via parse_gaussian (so the basis coefficients match exactly), is:
#
#     PySCF 2.13.0 RHF/cc-pV6Z spherical, Ne:  -128.5470611007 Eh
#
# Expect ~1e-9 agreement, as measured for f/g/h. If it disagrees, the suspect is the
# oracle path (spherical_recurrence.cpp), NOT normalized_pseudoinverse -- L=6
# delegates to the oracle and never touches the pseudoinverse.
%begin_control
    basis       cc-pV6Z
    calculation energy
    basis_type  spherical
%end_control

%begin_scf
    scf_type    rhf
    use_diis    .true.
    diis_dim    8
    guess       hcore
    max_cycles  300
    tol_energy  1.0e-10
    tol_density 1.0e-10
%end_scf

%begin_geom
    coord_type  cartesian
    coord_units angstrom
    use_symm    .false.
%end_geom

%begin_coords
1
0   1
Ne    0.000000     0.000000     0.000000
%end_coords
